    "updated_at": "2026-10-14T10:25:22Z",
    "body": "I think the fix is to expose the selected resource's dimensions directly. Reversing the density correction with the source size doesn't work anymore with #13046: `naturalWidth` is exactly the `sizes` value for images selected with a `w` descriptor.\n\nMaybe `pixelWidth`/`pixelHeight` on both `HTMLImageElement` and `HTMLVideoElement`: the current resource's natural dimensions in image (or video) pixels. That's after orientation (and, for video, aspect-ratio correction), but before any density correction (from `srcset` or EXIF resolution). They'd be 0 if the image isn't available or has no natural dimensions.\n\n`naturalWidth` and `videoWidth` would both stay the natural size in CSS pixels. For video that's the same as the pixel size today, since video has no density. If video gets `srcset`/`sizes` (#10379), `videoWidth` would become density-corrected like `naturalWidth`, and `pixelWidth` would mean the same thing on both elements.\n\nExposing the current source size (#6532) is also useful, for a different use case. Together, a `sizes` linter could check these without parsing `sizes` itself:\n\n- `currentSizes` vs `clientWidth`: is `sizes` accurate?\n- `pixelWidth` vs `clientWidth × devicePixelRatio`: is the served image too big or too small?\n- `pixelWidth` vs the selected candidate's `w`: is the `w` descriptor accurate?",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
