    "updated_at": "2026-10-14T14:52:41Z",
    "body": "@noamr\n> (bikeshedding) How about `sourceWidth` and `sourceHeight`?\n\nI'm OK with other names. But `naturalWidth` is also the selected source's width, so a name that says how this differs from `naturalWidth` and `videoWidth` seems better. How about `resourceWidth`?\n\n> Or `selectedSource{Width|Height}` and `actualSource{Width|Height}` if we want to be explicit about what was selected by the srcset algorithm and what actually arrived?\n\nExposing the selected `w` would allow checking whether the `w` descriptor is accurate. A `currentDensity` would, too (`currentDensity × currentSizes` vs `resourceWidth`, with `currentSizes` from #6532), and it also works for `x` descriptors.\n\n@austinh In CSS, [\"intrinsic size\"](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-sizing-3/#intrinsic-sizes) means something else now (sizing based on content). Before that, \"intrinsic dimensions\" was the old name for what CSS now calls \"natural dimensions\", which mostly matches `naturalWidth`. Either way, `intrinsicWidth` doesn't say how it differs from `naturalWidth`, and the two could be mistaken for synonyms.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
