    "body": ">  How about `object{Width|Height}` as in `object-fit`?\n\nThere are many \"object\"s, I don't think most people will think of `object-fit` first...\n\n>  But `{image|video}{Width|Height}` is also OK.\n\nThis could work. Then `imageWidth` and `videoWidth` could be defined to be pretty similar, and if `video` gets `srcset`, we can add `naturalWidth` for the density-corrected CSS pixel width.\n\nThe spec says `videoWidth` is in CSS pixels, and allows format-defined scaling (resolution, MP4 `tkhd` presentation size), but browsers in practice report video pixels after aspect ratio correction (#13106). I think it should be defined that way, so it stays uncorrected if video gets `srcset`.\n\nPer local testing with Claude: Firefox, Chrome and Safari don't apply the container-declared size (MP4 tkhd, WebM DisplayWidth/DisplayHeight) other than for aspect ratio, once the first frame is decoded (except AV1 in MP4 in Firefox, which looks like a bug (edit: filed [bug 2084400](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2084400))).",
